 "finish_summary": "Optimized priority32 by eliminating a redundant top-level combinational mux: since the original idx_0 priority chain already evaluates to 5'd0 in all \"default\" branches (including when a_i is entirely zero), the final `hit_0 ? {1'b1, idx_0} : 6'd0` mux was logically redundant and could be replaced with a plain concatenation `{hit_0, idx_0}`. This was verified functionally identical via Verilator simulation (PASS) and formally proven equivalent to the reference via Yosys sequential equivalence checking (PROVEN). I also explored a balanced binary-tree leading-one-detector restructuring (O(log32) depth vs the O(32) cascaded chain) hoping to improve timing further, but post-route results showed it was consistently worse in both area and WNS than the simplified chain, since the original cascaded-ternary structure was already well-optimized by ABC's technology mapping and the achievable WNS is largely fixed by non-logic overhead (~7.6-7.7ns regardless of encoder microarchitecture) rather than the encoder's logic depth. The best verified result was the simplified chain design: area 201.096 um^2, WNS 7.66747 ns, score 76.473604, an improvement of +0.227 over the baseline reference's post-route score of 76.24689. This is the result I'm finalizing.",
